  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nZonal flow aloft will remain in place across the region through \nearly next week. Wildfire smoke originating from OR, WA, and ID will \ncontinue to overspread much of the region as a result. Near to a \ntouch above average temperatures are forecast over this timeframe, \nwith largely dry conditions continuing. A few showers or \nthunderstorms here or there cannot be ruled out, but they look to be \nvery isolated. \n\nToward the middle of next week ensembles are in favor of a cooler \nstretch with opportunities for some rain. Areas near the \nInternational Border will be most favored, while areas near the ID \nborder look less favored. -AM\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nRain and temperatures next week:\n\nWednesday and Thursday the probability for a quarter of an inch of \nrain is greater than 50% across the Hi-Line, slowly decreasing as \nyou head further south toward Southwest Montana. By Thursday the \nprobability for a high temperature of less than 70 F is 50% or \ngreater over much of the plains. Southwest valleys look to be a \nlittle bit warmer, hence the probability for a 70 F or lower high \ntemperature on Thursday is 30% or less. -AM",
